## Failure diagnosis (artifact-cited)

`coo\live\20260905-ms01-scripts-inventory.execute.live.log` shows the executor died at a CLI permission gate: `"Exec ended early: insufficient permission to proceed. Re-run with --auto medium or --auto high."` with `num_turns: 0`, `duration_ms: 269877`. The executor never took a single agentic turn — no wrong plan, no wrong artifacts, no work product exists to critique. This is not transient: the loop's execute stage (`coo-loop-v4.sh`, do_execute) invokes `droid execute exec -o json -f` with **no `--auto` flag**, so the identical gate reproduces on every re-fire.

## Why not RELOOP

The failure is environmental and first-of-contract, but RELOOP is defined as *same executor session continued* — and that mechanism is infeasible here for two mechanical reasons: (1) the session id was never persisted (`coo-loop-v4.sh` greps exec.log for `"sessionId"` while the JSON emits `"session_id"`, so no `executor_session:` line landed in the tangent file); (2) even a resumed session would be relaunched through the same flagless execute invocation and hit the same deterministic gate. RELOOP would burn cycle 2 of 3 to arrive at this same conclusion. A prior assessor attempt (committed `f32fd59`, crashed at the verdict-ingest step) independently reached the same conclusion before its output was recorded by the loop; this retry confirms it.

